'''''Aeonium urbicum''''' is a [[succulent]] species of [[flowering plant]] in the family [[Crassulaceae]]. It is [[Endemism|endemic]] to [[Tenerife]], one of the [[Canary Islands]], where it grows on the north of the island from Teno point to the Anaga peninsula.<ref name=Bramwell>{{cite book|last1=Bramwell|first1=D.|last2=Bramwell|first2=Z.|year=2001|title=Wild flowers of the Canary Islands|publisher=Editorial Rueda|location=Madrid, Spain|ISBN=84-7207-129-4}}</ref>{{rp|174}} Until 1999, it was also considered a resident of [[La Gomera]], another Canary Island, but that year the populations on that island were considered a new species and named ''[[Aeonium appendiculatum]]''.<ref name=ban>{{citation |author=Bañares Baudet, Á. |year=1999 |title=Notes on the taxonomy of ''Aeonium urbicum'' and ''A. appendiculatum'' sp. nova (Crassulaceae) |journal=Willdenowia |volume=29 |pages=95–103 |doi=10.3372/wi.29.2908|doi-access=free }}</ref>

''Aeonium urbicum'' was also divided into varieties, var. ''urbicum'' and var. ''meridionale''.<ref name=ban/>

